aquinas-ontological-objection.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r1 has "known_on_terms"; aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r1.2 and aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r1.3 have nothing from it; they differ when nothing is true, which the second allows [in 2 of 3 runs]
aquinas-ontological-objection.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r1, to: Suppose "as soon as the signification of the word "God" is understood, it is at once seen that God exists" is false. Is S4, "But as soon as the signification of the word "God" is understood, it is at once seen that God exists.", true then? [in 1 of 3 runs]
aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r1 has "signifies_greatest ∧ actual_greater ∧ exists_mentally → exists_actually"; aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r1.2 has nothing from it; they differ when only "by the word "God" is signified that thing than which nothing greater can be conceived", "that which exists actually and mentally is greater than that which exists only mentally" and "as soon as the word "God" is understood, God exists mentally" are true, which the second allows; aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r1.3 has "signifies_greatest ∧ actual_greater ∧ exists_mentally → exists_actually" and "signifies_greatest ∧ exists_mentally ∧ exists_actually → known_on_terms"
aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r1 and aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r1.3 take for granted "if (by the word "God" is signified that thing than which nothing greater can be conceived and that which exists actually and mentally is greater than that which exists only mentally and as soon as the word "God" is understood, God exists mentally), then God exists actually"; aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r1.2 does not [in 2 of 3 runs]
aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r1 and aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r1.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (by the word "God" is signified that thing than which nothing greater can be conceived and that which exists actually and mentally is greater than that which exists only mentally and as soon as the word "God" is understood, God exists mentally), then God exists actually"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument. [in 2 of 3 runs]
aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r1: argue/readings: U2: aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r1 and aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r1.3 take for granted "if (by the word "God" is signified that thing than which nothing greater can be conceived and as soon as the word "God" is understood, God exists mentally and God exists actually), then as soon as the signification of the word "God" is understood, it is at once seen that God exists"; aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r1.2 does not [in 1 of 3 runs]
aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r1: argue/answered: U2: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r1 and aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r1.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (by the word "God" is signified that thing than which nothing greater can be conceived and as soon as the word "God" is understood, God exists mentally and God exists actually), then as soon as the signification of the word "God" is understood, it is at once seen that God exists"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument. [in 1 of 3 runs]
aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r1: argue/verdicts: valid as aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r1, aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r1.3 read it, not as the others do: which reading is right decides the verdict [in 1 of 3 runs]
